# Usage
########

# passivedns
See doc/INSTALL for the Ubuntu 26.04 build.

root@localhost: ./passivedns -i eth1 -l /var/log/passivedns.log

# pdns2db.py
root@localhost: sudo apt install python3-pymysql

search-pdns.pl still needs the Perl database modules:

root@localhost: sudo apt install libdatetime-perl libdate-simple-perl libdbd-mysql-perl

First you need to add a user to the MySQL or MariaDB server. Grant
SELECT, CREATE, INSERT, UPDATE, ALTER, and INDEX on the pdns database.
ALTER and INDEX are used once, when an older varchar(255) ANSWER column
is widened so a long TXT fits.

mysql> CREATE DATABASE pdns;
mysql> CREATE USER 'pdns'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'choose-a-password';
mysql> GRANT SELECT, CREATE, INSERT, UPDATE, ALTER, INDEX ON pdns.* TO 'pdns'@'localhost';
mysql> FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

Then you need to start up pdns2db.py

root@localhost: export PDNS_DB_PASSWORD='choose-a-password'
root@localhost: python3 pdns2db.py --file /var/log/passivedns.log

The web page in www/ is a local demo. It reads the same PDNS_DB_PASSWORD,
PDNS_DB_PORT, and PDNS_DB_TABLE. Set PDNS_WEB_TOKEN as well if that page
is reachable beyond localhost. The table definition is sql/pdns.sql.
Report a vulnerability through SECURITY.md.

# Old School archiving of passivedns.log
# While passivedns is running;
$ mkdir /var/log/passivedns-archive/
$ mv /var/log/passivedns.log /var/log/passivedns-archive/
$ gzip -S ".`date +%s`.gz" /var/log/passivedns-archive/passivedns.log 
# Searching
$ zgrep "gamelinux.org" /var/log/passivedns-archive/*

